From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] xcrypt: guard getspnam call with HAVE_GETSPNAM

xcrypt.c's shadow_pw() guards the getspnam() call with
HAVE_SHADOW_H alone, but that macro only reflects the presence of
<shadow.h> (and struct spwd). On OHOS the sysroot ships shadow.h
with struct spwd but no getspnam declaration or symbol, so
configure sets ac_cv_func_getspnam=no and leaves HAVE_GETSPNAM
undefined. The call then resolves to an implicit int-returning
declaration, and clang rejects assigning that to struct spwd *:

  xcrypt.c:140:15: error: incompatible integer to pointer conversion
  initializing 'struct spwd *' with an expression of type 'int'

Tighten the guard to require HAVE_GETSPNAM as well; when it is
undefined the function falls through to the existing fallback that
returns pw->pw_passwd. auth.c and auth-shadow.c already gate their
getspnam usage on USE_SHADOW, which configure leaves unset on OHOS,
so only xcrypt.c needs this fix.

---
 openbsd-compat/xcrypt.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/openbsd-compat/xcrypt.c b/openbsd-compat/xcrypt.c
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/openbsd-compat/xcrypt.c
+++ b/openbsd-compat/xcrypt.c
@@ -135,7 +135,7 @@ shadow_pw(struct passwd *pw)
 {
 	char *pw_password = pw->pw_passwd;

-# if defined(HAVE_SHADOW_H) && !defined(DISABLE_SHADOW)
+# if defined(HAVE_SHADOW_H) && defined(HAVE_GETSPNAM) && !defined(DISABLE_SHADOW)
 	struct spwd *spw = getspnam(pw->pw_name);

 	if (spw != NULL)
--
2.39.1
